  "question": {
    "text": "If \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are the roots of the equation \\(x^2+x+1=0\\), then the equation whose roots are \\(\\alpha^{19}\\) and \\(\\beta^7\\) is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "cH4u4",
      "text": "\\(x^2-x-1=0\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cm4AL",
      "text": "\\(x^2-x+1=0\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "P39fk",
      "text": "\\(x^2+x-1=0\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FTDwH",
      "text": "\\(x^2+x+1=0\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hints: \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are the roots of \\(x^2+x+1=0\\)\n\\(\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\alpha=\\omega & \\beta=\\omega^2 \\\\\n\\alpha^{19}=\\omega & \\beta^7=\\omega^2 \\\\\nx^2-\\left(\\alpha^{19}+\\beta^7\\right) x+\\alpha^{19} \\beta^7=0\n\\end{array}\\)\nThou,\n\\(\\begin{aligned}\n& x^2-\\left(\\omega+\\omega^2\\right) x+\\omega \\cdot \\omega^2=0 \\\\\n& x^2+x+1=0\n\\end{aligned}\\)",
    "image": null
  },
